@startuml
title Key Expiry Lifecycle - Redis-like Server

' The life of one key with respect to its deadline (FR-006).
' "Due" is a key whose deadline has passed but which the sweep has not yet
' removed: it is still in memory, but no command can observe it.

[*] --> Persistent : key created\n(SET, LPUSH, HSET, SADD ...)

state Persistent : no deadline\nTTL = -1
state Volatile   : deadline in the future\nTTL = seconds left
state Due        : deadline passed, still in memory\nlooks missing to every command
state Gone       : not in the keyspace\nTTL = -2

Persistent --> Volatile : EXPIRE / PEXPIRE / EXPIREAT / PEXPIREAT\nSET ... EX / PX / EXAT / PXAT
Volatile   --> Volatile : a new deadline replaces the old one
Volatile   --> Persistent : PERSIST\nSET without KEEPTTL, MSET
Volatile   --> Volatile : INCR, APPEND, LPUSH, HSET ...\nand SET ... KEEPTTL keep the deadline

Persistent --> Gone : deadline already past\n(amount <= 0, or EXPIREAT in the past)
Volatile   --> Gone : deadline already past\n(amount <= 0, or EXPIREAT in the past)

Volatile --> Due : the clock passes the deadline
Due --> Gone : lazy: the next command that\nlooks the key up removes it
Due --> Gone : active: the sweep removes it\n(every 100 ms, up to 1000 keys,\noldest deadline first)

Persistent --> Gone : DEL, FLUSHDB
Volatile   --> Gone : DEL, FLUSHDB
Gone --> [*]

note right of Due
  Counted in expired_keys when removed.
  DBSIZE and INFO keyspace "expires" still
  include it until it is removed.
end note

note bottom of Gone
  Removal by expiry is logged as DEL in the
  append-only file (--appendonly).
end note

@enduml
